          <SocialProof users="10.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-they-work" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How Each One Works</a>
          <a href="#surface-vs-penetrating" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Coating vs Penetrating</a>
          <a href="#hydrostatic" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Hydrostatic Pressure Problem</a>
          <a href="#substrate" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Which Walls Each Suits</a>
          <a href="#application" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Applying Each One</a>
          <a href="#expectations" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. What Neither Will Fix</a>
          <a href="#choosing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Choosing Between Them</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          These two get compared because they both promise a dry basement, but they work on opposite principles. DRYLOK is a coating that blocks water at the surface; RadonSeal is a penetrating sealer that reacts inside the pores of the concrete. Which one is right depends entirely on where the water is coming from and what your walls are made of.
        </p>

        <div id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Each One Actually Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>DRYLOK</strong> is a waterproofing masonry coating — essentially a thick, filled paint. Applied to bare masonry it bridges the pores and builds a continuous film on the surface, and that film is what stops water passing through. Its own literature rates it to hold back a stated amount of hydrostatic pressure, which is a claim about the film's adhesion and strength.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>RadonSeal</strong> is a penetrating sealer based on sodium silicate chemistry. It soaks into the concrete and reacts with free lime and calcium hydroxide inside the pores, forming a crystalline structure that narrows the capillary paths from within. Nothing remains on the surface — the concrete looks unchanged afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            That difference produces every practical distinction between them. A surface film can be beautiful, can be tinted, and can peel. A penetrating sealer cannot peel because there is nothing on the surface to detach, but it also cannot bridge a gap, hide a stain, or change how the wall looks.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also changes what happens over time. A coating's life is the life of its bond to the substrate. A penetrating sealer's reaction products are part of the concrete, so its life is effectively the life of the wall — provided it got in far enough in the first place, which depends on the concrete being porous and clean when applied.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Distinction That Decides It</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">DRYLOK stops water at the surface. RadonSeal narrows the paths inside.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If your wall is sound and damp from capillary moisture, the penetrating approach addresses the mechanism. If you want a visible, tintable, uniform finish on masonry, only the coating does that. Neither substitutes for the other, and neither fixes water arriving under pressure through a crack.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="surface-vs-penetrating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Coating vs Penetrating, Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laid side by side, the trade-offs are fairly clean.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">DRYLOK (coating)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">RadonSeal (penetrating)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Where it sits</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Film on the surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reacted inside the pores</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Appearance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Visible, white or tintable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No visible change</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Can it peel?</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, if the bond fails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No — nothing on the surface</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bridges hairline pores</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Narrows but does not bridge</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Works on painted walls</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only on bare masonry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No — must reach bare concrete</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Can be painted over</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, once cured</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lifespan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Life of the bond</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Life of the concrete</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Reduces radon entry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Incidentally</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Marketed for it specifically</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The row that catches most people is 'works on painted walls'. Both products need bare, clean masonry. A previously painted basement wall has to be stripped or the coating is bonding to old paint rather than to the wall — and the old paint is then the weakest link, which is why so many DRYLOK failures are really paint failures underneath.
          </p>

          <p>
            The radon row deserves a caveat. Sealing concrete reduces one entry path for soil gas, and RadonSeal markets that explicitly, but no sealer is a radon mitigation system. If you have tested high, the answer is sub-slab depressurisation installed by a certified contractor; a sealer is at best a supporting measure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hydrostatic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Hydrostatic Pressure Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where both products are oversold by the people who buy them, rather than by the manufacturers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydrostatic pressure is water in the soil pressing against the outside of your foundation. When the water table rises or drainage fails, that pressure can be substantial, and it will find any path available — through pores, through the cove joint where wall meets floor, through cracks, and through any weakness in a coating.
          </p>

          <p>
            DRYLOK publishes a pressure rating, and within that rating on sound masonry it does hold. But the film is only as good as its weakest point, and a pinhole, a thin spot or a missed patch concentrates all the pressure there. This is the classic failure: a wall that stayed dry for two seasons and then blistered in one wet spring.
          </p>

          <p>
            RadonSeal narrows capillary paths but does not resist bulk water under pressure at all. Water arriving through a crack or the cove joint will continue to arrive, because the sealer has done nothing to that pathway — it addresses moisture wicking through sound concrete, which is a different phenomenon.
          </p>

          <p>
            If water actively enters after rain, neither product is your answer. That is a drainage problem — gutters, grading, a failed footing drain — and our <a href="/hydrostatic-pressure-in-basement/">hydrostatic pressure guide</a> covers diagnosing it. Sealing a wall that water is pushing through relocates the problem rather than solving it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="substrate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Walls Each One Suits
          </h2>

          <p>
            The wall material matters as much as the water source, and it is the part people skip.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Poured Concrete</h3>

          <p>
            Both work. Penetrating sealers perform particularly well here because poured concrete has consistent porosity and plenty of free lime to react with, especially if it is relatively young. DRYLOK also bonds well provided the surface is clean and not sealed by curing compound or laitance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Concrete Block</h3>

          <p>
            DRYLOK is usually the better answer. Block has hollow cores and mortar joints, and its porosity varies enormously between the block face and the joint — a coating spans that inconsistency where a penetrating sealer treats each material differently. Block walls also benefit from the film bridging fine surface pores.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stone or Rubble Foundations</h3>

          <p>
            Neither is a reliable answer. Old stone foundations move, the mortar is often lime-based and soft, and a rigid coating cracks along every joint. These walls generally need repointing, interior drainage, or a managed approach that accepts some moisture rather than trying to seal it out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Previously Painted or Sealed Walls</h3>

          <p>
            Neither will work as intended. The coating bonds to the old paint rather than the wall; the penetrating sealer cannot get through the paint at all. Strip back to bare masonry, or accept that you are repainting rather than waterproofing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Applying Each One
          </h2>

          <p>
            The application requirements differ enough to be a factor in the decision, particularly on a big wall.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">DRYLOK</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">RadonSeal</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surface prep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bare, clean, no efflorescence</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bare, clean, porous, no sealers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Method</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stiff brush or roller, worked in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sprayed or rolled, kept wet</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Coats</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two, second at right angles</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two or more wet-on-wet to refusal</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Coverage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">75-100 sq ft per gallon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200+ sq ft per gallon</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cure before test</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-48 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Days; full reaction takes weeks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Visible result</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Obvious white or tinted film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            DRYLOK's requirement to work the first coat into the pores with a stiff brush rather than rolling it on is the step most commonly skipped, and skipping it is the most common cause of later peeling. The second coat can be rolled; the first one has to be scrubbed in.
          </p>

          <p>
            RadonSeal's requirement is different: the surface must stay wet long enough for the material to penetrate, which means applying to refusal rather than to a set coverage figure. On dense concrete that may be two applications; on porous block it can be more. Removing efflorescence first matters for both, since salt deposits block penetration and ruin adhesion equally.
          </p>

          <p>
            One shared warning: neither should be applied to a wall that is actively wet. Both need a dry substrate, which on a chronically damp wall means fixing the source first and then waiting — often weeks — before sealing anything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="expectations" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Neither Product Will Fix
          </h2>

          <p>
            Being blunt about this saves people a weekend and a few hundred dollars.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Water entering through cracks.</strong> A structural or settlement crack is a pathway, not a porosity problem. It needs routing and filling, or polyurethane injection for an active leak. Sealing over it works until the next time the crack moves.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Water at the cove joint.</strong> Where the wall meets the floor is where hydrostatic water most often arrives, and neither product addresses it. That is an interior drainage job.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A failed exterior drainage system.</strong> Blocked gutters, downpipes discharging at the foundation, ground sloping toward the house, or a collapsed footing drain. These are the actual cause in most wet basements, and they are cheaper to fix than anything done on the inside.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Humidity.</strong> A basement that is damp rather than wet — condensation on cold surfaces, musty smell, no visible water — has an air problem, not a wall problem. A dehumidifier addresses that and a sealer does not; our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">dehumidifier guide</a> covers sizing for below-grade spaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both products are legitimate for what they do: reducing moisture transmission through sound masonry. Neither is a waterproofing system, and a wet basement usually needs one.
          </p>
        </div>

          <p>
            Cost is worth a brief mention because it rarely decides this. Both land in the same broad range once you account for coverage: DRYLOK covers far less per gallon but needs two coats on bare block, while a penetrating sealer covers more ground but is applied to refusal, which on porous walls means more material than the label suggests. For a typical basement the difference between them is smaller than the cost of stripping old paint off the walls first.
          </p>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Choosing Between Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            With the caveats above understood, the choice is reasonably clear.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Your Situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Choose</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Block walls, want a finished look</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">DRYLOK</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bridges variable porosity; tintable</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Poured concrete, damp but sound</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">RadonSeal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Treats capillary moisture at the mechanism</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Want to paint the walls afterwards</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Either, DRYLOK if colour matters</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Both accept paint once cured</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concerned about radon as well</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">RadonSeal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Formulated and marketed for soil gas</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wall was previously painted</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Neither until stripped</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Both need bare masonry</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Active water entry after rain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Neither — fix drainage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sealing relocates the problem</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you want one recommendation for the most common case — a block basement that is damp, musty and cosmetically tired, with no active leaks — DRYLOK is the more satisfying purchase, because it addresses the moisture and transforms how the space looks in one weekend.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a poured concrete basement that is structurally sound and merely transmitting moisture, RadonSeal is the more durable answer, precisely because there is no film to fail. You will not be able to see that you did anything, which is the point.
          </p>

          <p>
            And in both cases, do the outside first. Clearing gutters, extending downpipes and correcting the grade around a foundation costs very little and resolves more wet basements than any product applied to the inside of the wall. Our <a href="/wet-basement-solutions/">wet basement guide</a> works through that order of operations.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Either Over Painted Walls</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both need bare masonry. A coating bonds to the old paint rather than the wall, making the paint the weakest link, and a penetrating sealer cannot get through paint at all. Most DRYLOK failures are old paint failures underneath.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Instead of Fixing Drainage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Blocked gutters, downpipes discharging at the foundation and ground sloping toward the house cause more wet basements than porous walls do. Sealing the inside while water is pushing on the outside relocates the problem to the next weak point.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rolling DRYLOK's First Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The first coat must be worked into the pores with a stiff brush, not rolled. Skipping that is the single most common cause of a coating that blisters and peels a season or two later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying to a Damp Wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both need a dry substrate. On a chronically damp wall that means fixing the source first and then waiting — often weeks — before applying anything. Sealing a wet wall traps the moisture behind the treatment.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Sealer to Handle Radon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealing reduces one entry path for soil gas, and that is all. If a test comes back high, the remedy is sub-slab depressurisation by a certified contractor; treat any sealer as a supporting measure rather than the mitigation.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between DRYLOK and RadonSeal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                DRYLOK is a waterproofing coating that forms a film on the surface of masonry and blocks water there. RadonSeal is a penetrating sodium silicate sealer that soaks in and reacts with the concrete to narrow the capillary pores from inside. One is visible and can peel; the other is invisible and cannot.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for a basement, DRYLOK or RadonSeal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the wall and the problem. For concrete block walls where you also want a finished look, DRYLOK — it bridges variable porosity and can be tinted. For sound poured concrete that is simply transmitting moisture, RadonSeal is more durable because there is no film to fail.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can DRYLOK stop water under hydrostatic pressure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Within its published rating, on sound masonry, it holds. But the film is only as strong as its weakest point, so a pinhole or thin spot concentrates the pressure and fails. If water actively enters after rain, the real fix is drainage — gutters, grading or the footing drain — not a coating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does RadonSeal actually reduce radon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sealing concrete closes one entry path for soil gas, and RadonSeal is formulated and marketed for that. It is not a mitigation system. If a radon test comes back high, sub-slab depressurisation installed by a certified contractor is the remedy; a sealer is a supporting measure at best.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I apply either over painted basement walls?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Both require bare, clean masonry. A coating applied over old paint bonds to the paint rather than the wall, and the paint then becomes the failure point. A penetrating sealer cannot pass through paint at all. Strip back to bare masonry first or you are decorating rather than waterproofing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over DRYLOK or RadonSeal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, both, once fully cured. DRYLOK is also available tinted, which saves a step if you want colour. With RadonSeal the concrete looks unchanged afterwards, so a finish coat is purely cosmetic and can be applied whenever the reaction is complete.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will either fix a crack that leaks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A crack is a pathway rather than a porosity problem, and it needs routing and filling, or polyurethane injection if it is actively leaking. Sealing over a crack works until the next time it moves, which on a settlement crack may be the next wet season.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

